{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:anupreksa", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "anupreksa", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The twelve reflections are the ladder to meditation: by contemplating impermanence, the lack of refuge, the round of births, aloneness and the rest the mind becomes detached and fit for meditation.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:anupreksa"], "concepts": ["cpt:twelve-anupreksas"], "practices": ["prc:anupreksa"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Section-level ref (early sargas).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:asad-dhyana", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "asad-dhyana", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Meditation and mantra used to harm, attract, subjugate or kill others is evil meditation (asad-dhyāna); it belongs to the sorrowful and cruel meditations and binds karma.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "ethics"], "concepts": ["cpt:jain-mantra-theory"], "obstacles": ["obs:asad-dhyana", "obs:arta-raudra-dhyana"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Content recalled in outline; section not located.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:dhyatr", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "dhyatr", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The meditator should be detached, of steady vows, free of the passions, knowing the realities, and should choose a solitary, undisturbed place — a mountain, cave, forest or temple — and a steady posture.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:dhyatr"], "practices": ["prc:dharma-dhyana"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Section-level ref.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:padastha", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "padastha", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Word-based meditation contemplates the letters and sacred mantras — the vowels on a sixteen-petalled lotus, the syllable 'arhaṃ', the praṇava, the homage to the five supreme beings — as pure objects leading the mind inward.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "sound"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice", "sound-language"], "terms": ["trm:padastha", "trm:arham"], "practices": ["prc:padastha-dhyana"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Section-level ref.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:pindastha", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "pindastha", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "In embodied meditation the yogin performs the five concentrations — earth, fire, wind, water and the essential — burning the lotus of the eight karmas with the fire arising from the syllable 'arhaṃ' and finally seeing his own self as the pure, omniscient Siddha.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "sound"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:pindastha", "trm:agneyi-dharana"], "concepts": ["cpt:five-dharanas-jain", "cpt:four-dhyeyas"], "practices": ["prc:pindastha-dhyana", "prc:five-dharanas-jain"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Section-level ref.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:pranayama", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "pranayama", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Breath-control may steady the body and reveal omens, but those who pursue it for powers fall into distress; it agitates the mind and is not the cause of liberation, which is reached by the mind's own stillness.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["body-breath"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "dispute"], "practices": ["prc:pranayama"], "obstacles": ["obs:labdhi-attachment"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "disputes": ["dsp:pranayama-and-liberation"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "restricted": true, "notes": "The text's own warnings; section-level ref (the prāṇāyāma sargas); content recalled in outline.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:jnanarnava:rupastha-rupatita", "source": "src:jnanarnava", "location": {"ref": "rupastha-rupatita", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Form-based meditation contemplates the omniscient Arhat in his glory; formless meditation contemplates the bodiless Siddha of pure consciousness, until meditator and meditated are not experienced as two.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice", "ultimate"], "terms": ["trm:rupastha", "trm:rupatita"], "practices": ["prc:rupastha-dhyana", "prc:rupatita-dhyana"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:subhacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Section-level ref.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
